#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082913 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97802ae1-4976-5fdf-bbe1-bcd001d5e53a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Sum of a Geometric Series Given the First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "2cd68908-78fc-56a2-9304-2aa5db4176c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4190" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the first term is $-3$ and the common ratio is $2$?" :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N =\\dfrac{a_1(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1= -3$ and $r = 2$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= (-3(1 - 2^{N}))/(1 - 2) \\\\\n&= ((-3))/((-1))(1 - 2^{N}) \\\\\n&= 3(1 - 2^{N})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c17552f7-0720-5e45-9c79-7db73e332045" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Sum of a Geometric Series Given the First Few Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "45d0d5b6-71a3-5b09-a10f-1e4a62ed287a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4191" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of the geometric series $2+8+32+\\cdots \\:$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the common ratio $r$ for this geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{2} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{a_1 (1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1=2$ and $r=4$. Substituting these values into the formula for $S_N$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= (a_{1}(1 - r^{N}))/(1 - r) \\\\\n&= (2(1 - 4^{N}))/(1 - 4) \\\\\n&= (2(1 - 4^{N}))/((-3)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}(1 - 4^{N}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}(4^{N} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd32a853-e0ec-5d56-bd6b-1c2e0e3249f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Sum of a Geometric Series Given an Arbitrary Term and the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "10909636-a083-5a1c-bf88-f1334ef69666" :question/math-academy-id "e-4192" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $3$rd term is $18$ and the common ratio is $3$?" :question/worked-solution "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine $a_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1}r^{n - 1} \\\\\n18 &= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{(3 - 1)} \\\\\n18 &= 9a_{1} \\\\\na_{1} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{a_1 (1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1=2$ and $r=3$. Substituting our values into the formula for $S_N$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= (2(1 - 3^{N}))/(1 - 3) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{2}(1 - 3^{N}) \\\\\n&= 3^{N} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
